somewhere in retained nodes (so we cannot remove the last node providing a required symbol). (3) we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (presumably because then those children would be lost). But if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree? Typically in tree reduction, removing a node means its children become children of its parent (or are discarded). The specification says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies we consider removing a node only if it has no children that are retained, i.e., its entire subtree is removable. And also we must ensure after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., some node with that symbol remains). So we need to find a maximal set of removable nodes to delete, respecting these constraints.\n\nAlso ordering: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests a traversal order from deepest to shallowest (post-order). \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So when multiple nodes at same depth, sort by node ID (string sort? Probably lexicographic). \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we process nodes in that order, and for each candidate, we check if removing it would still keep all required symbols (considering other nodes that have those symbols and are not removed). Also we must ensure the node itself is removable (removable=true) and not a required symbol, and has no children that are retained (i.e., all its descendants that are not removed? But we process in post-order so when we consider a node, its children have already been considered for removal. So if a child was retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it has retained children. So in the algorithm, we can keep a set of retained nodes (initially all nodes? Actually we start with all nodes, then we decide which to remove. Alternatively, we can process nodes in reverse depth order and for each node, if it is removable and not a required symbol and all its children are either already removed or non-existent (i.e., it has no children in the retained set), then we can attempt to remove it, but only if after removal, each required symbol is still present in the retained set (including nodes not yet processed or already decided to retain). Since we are processing deepest first, when considering a node, all its descendants have been processed; so if any descendant was retained (not removed), that means the node has retained children and cannot be removed. So the condition \"node with retained children\" is automatically enforced if we only remove nodes with no children in retained set. So the algorithm: start with a set of all node IDs as \"pending\" or \"tentatively retained\". Then iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node id ascending. For each node, if it is removable and its symbol not in required_symbols and it has no children in the current retained set (i.e., all its children IDs are not in retained set; note children are from parent pointers, we need to build children mapping), then we test removal: we temporarily remove it (i.e., consider it removed) and check if for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol still in the retained set (excluding this node). If yes, we permanently remove it (add to removed list). Else we keep it (retain). At the end, we have retained_nodes (those not removed) and removed_nodes in order of removal. Return sorted retained_node_ids (alphabetically? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted? Probably sorted chronologically of removal? It says \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely sorted alphabetically or by ID? Usually \"sorted\" meaning lexicographic. And removal order is the order in which we removed nodes. The output schema has \"removed_node_ids\" (array) and \"retained_node_ids\" (array). The removal order should be the order of removal (the order we processed and decided to remove). The retained IDs sorted.\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. Build structures from case['nodes'].\n\nEdge cases: The root is not removable and its symbol 'root' is likely required (it's in required_symbols in examples), but we treat it as not removable anyway.\n\nAlso note: The required_symbols list may include symbols that appear multiple times. As long as at least one node with that symbol remains, it's ok.\n\nWe also need to raise ValueError for infeasible cases? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if after processing, some required symbol is missing (shouldn't happen because root is always there and required), but if input is malformed, we could raise. But given the fixtures, it's fine.\n\nWe should implement solve(case) to return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nNow write the code. We'll parse nodes into dict by id, build children mapping (list of child ids for each parent). We'll precompute node depth and symbol, removable, etc.\n\nSort nodes by depth descending, then node id ascending (string comparison). Iterate:\n\nretained = set(all_ids) initially. removed_order = [].\n\nFor each node in sorted_order:\n  if node.id not in retained? Actually we start with all retained, but we remove nodes as we go. So we check if node.id is in retained (it should be). \n  Then check conditions:\n    - removable == True\n    - symbol not in required_symbols\n    - no child in retained (i.e., for each child_id in children[node.id], child_id not in retained)\n  If pass, then test removal:\n    - temporarily remove: retained_without = retained - {node.id}\n    - for each req_sym in required_symbols:\n        found = any(n['symbol'] == req_sym for n in nodes if n['id'] in retained_without)\n        if not found: break (cannot remove)\n    - if all found, then permanently remove: retained = retained_without; removed_order.append(node.id)\n  else: keep (do nothing, node stays in retained).\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained) (string sort), removed_node_ids = removed_order (order of removal).\n\nReturn dict.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation key.\n\nMake sure to encode newlines and quotes properly. We'll write the Python code as a string.\n\nPotential edge: We need to ensure we don't remove a node whose parent is also being removed later? That's fine. The condition of no children in retained ensures no retained child. If we remove a node, its children (if any) must have already been removed because they would have been processed earlier (deeper depth) and if they were retained, they'd block parent removal. So this works.\n\nImportant: The condition \"all children are not retained\" means all children must have been removed. But if a node has no children, condition true.\n\nAlso, we need to consider that a node might have children that were never removable, so they remain retained and block removal. That's correct.\n\nNow produce final output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":2366,"total_tokens":4538,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1931},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}